Well, hmm. You could use the mithril to go about the dungeon and 
heal/feed your pets (and yourself) in between and take it off to cast 
some spells. It's a bit more trouble and, of course, a bit more 
dangerous, as you'll be standing around not wearing armor for a couple 
of turns and you can't really cast while combat is going on... Your 
choice, really.

> Question:  what weapon should I be using?  From reading some of your 
> suggestions, I think I'm really too much of a hand-to-hand fighter, 
> which doesn't make sense for a healer.  I have a composite bow, but it's 
> hard to find arrows.  Right now, I'm just killing things with my 
> scalpel.  :P

Takes for ever, I bet ;). Try having more pets. You'll be amazed how 
little trouble 3 dogs and 3 cats have in tearing a winged gargoyle 
appart :) Use meatballs to keep them fed (but don't starve in the 
process). If you have (as I see you have further down in your post) a 
magic whistle, transisitions from level to level shouldn't be all that 
bad...

Oh yeah, best thing you can get (prior to the quest), is a unicorn horn, 
I'd say. It's a two-handed weapon, so no shields, but if some monster 
manages to drag itself through your pets, it normally suffices to poke 
it with the unicorn horn.

> Question:  speaking of bows/arrows:  you can fire an arrow without 
> actually wielding your bow.  Is it doing negligeable damage?  If my bow 
> is my secondary weapon, is it useless (i.e. doesn't help with the firing 
> of the arrow)?  So, is the correct technique to have a H2H weapon in the 
> primary and a bow in the secondary, and then switch to the bow if the 
> monster is far enough, and then switch back to the H2H when the monster 
> is 2 squares away?  I know this sounds like an obvious thing to do, but 
> I've just never bothered, up 'till now, because of the hassle of getting 
> ammunition.  I suppose I could always get a sling and throw rocks, but 
> that seems... not heroic.  :)  Am I being incredibly lazy and silly?

You need to wield the launcher (sling, bow, crossbow...) in your weapon 
hand in order for it to work. A weapon in your off-hand will only do 
damage when you're #twoweaponing. Beating monsters with a bow is not a 
terribly good idea though (and I don't think Healers can #twoweapon anyway).

> Question:  is it my imagination, or can you safely throw a cursed 
> dagger?  Shouldn't it stick to your hand?

It won't stick to your hand. Nethack let's you throw stuff without 
wielding it, which is nice. It's still not so very safe, since the 
dagger might fly in the wrong direction and accidently hit someone and 
then what? Darts and arrows do this also, don't really know what happens 
when you throw a cursed scroll of light at something...)

